[IMP] google_calendar: multi calendar sync

The calendar.calendar model is extended with sync logic, sharing the base sync
class with events. Each calendar also holds its individual sync token so that we
only update when there is new data from Google.

## Sync Flow

1. Synchronize all calendars

Before starting the usual sync flow in 'res.users', we first make a call to
`/calendarList` to fetch all the user's calendars. A sync token for this call is
stored in the user settings, similar to the 'global' sync token before. All
calendars that do not have a google_id set are then posted to Google.

The calendars which are fetched are initially marked with a 'needs_import' flag.
They are hidden in the filters, and no events are imported into them until a
user decides to synchronize them. When new google calendars are fetched, we now
display a notification to inform the user that their import needs to be
explicitly enabled. The notification provides a redirect to the calendar list
view.

Unlike for events, the calendar endpoints do not return an 'updated' timestamp,
meaning we can't rely on last write date to determine which update wins in case
of a conflict - we have to decide which side is authoritative.
In this case -> Odoo

After this part of the request is done, we manually commit the changes. This is
because the google api methods are decorated with @after_commit so that we
don't send data if the sync process fails for some reason. We need all the
calendars to be synchronized when we sync events. That's why we call
`env.cr.commit()`

Small technical limitation:
Besides the events themselves, we currently only sync the name (summary) field
with google. When this is changed on google however, it does not invalidate the
sync token, meaning we are not notified of this change (changing some other
field on google, such as calendar color, correctly invalidates the sync token)

2. Synchronize the events

Google handles moving events between two calendars -> It returns a canceled
record for the original calendar, and a new one for the moved one. This means we
have to split the process into several parts instead of running the current
flow for each calendar:

2.1: Get events for all calendars from Google

Paths always used to be `.../primary/...` -> now `.../{calendar_id}/...`.
Otherwise the same as before.

2.2 Google -> Odoo

First, determine which should be active after the sync is done.
This is done to prevent canceling events which were moved in Google - E.g.:
* event1 was moved from cal1 to cal2
* when we iterate over the calendars, cal1 tells us the event was canceled
* if the event with the same id is still active somewhere, it means it was moved
and not canceled, se we leave it be
* cal2 then tells us the new calendar so we can update the event

When synchronizing the primary calendar, sync events from their primary
calendar plus the events where the user is an attendee -> all events matching
the original domain which are not in their secondary calendars

2.3 Odoo -> Google

This part is mostly unchanged from the previous flow, with calendar ids added to
the api route paths just like before

## Changing the event owner

Changing the organizer of an event transfers the event to their calendar.

If the event is moved to another user's calendar, we should delete the event
from the original owner's Google calendar and let the new owner synchronize it
to ensure the owner is correct on Google. This also prevents potential access
right issues if we tried to simply move the event to someone else's calendar.

## Allow events without attendees

Before, when we imported events from google which had no attendees, we always
added the current user as an attendee. This was because we filtered events based
on attendees/partner_ids, and an event without any would always be hidden in the
calendar view.

Events from shared google calendars often don't have attendees and are readonly.
(e.g. public holidays, or any calendar shared with the 'freeBusyReader' role)
In odoo, the user can typically edit events they attend, and as such could edit
these readonly events, causing them to desync.

Now we can filter based on calendars and show events without attendees, so we no
longer add attendees when importing events without them.

## Event user matching

The organizer field in the fetched google event record can only be relied on for
primary calendars. For shared calendars, the organizer email is always the ID of
the calendar. The organizer has 'self: True' even if the event was created by
another user. For example, if user A creates an event in their primary calendar,
the object will look like this:

"creator": {
    "email": "A@gmail.com",
    "self": true
},
"organizer": {
    "email": "A@gmail.com",
    "self": true
},

An event which the user was invited to and appears in my primary calendar looks
like this:

"creator": {
    "email": "B@gmail.com",
},
"organizer": {
    "email": "B@gmail.com",
},

If someone creates an event in a secondary calendar they can write on,
regardless of if they are the data owner, the object looks like this:

"creator": {
    "email": "A@gmail.com"
},
"organizer": {
    "email": "20f3...8b08@group.calendar.google.com",
    "displayName": "123",
    "self": true
},

In the example above, "A@gmail.com" created the event in a shared calendar and
they are attributed as the creator, but the `"self": true` flag is missing

If we still relied on organizer as before, all events from secondary calendars
would be attributed to env.user, regardless of their access rights to the
calendar.

This is because in google, unlike Odoo, the ownership of an event is decided
only based on which calendar it belongs to, and the calendar's ACL access rights

New approach:

1. When determining the calendar owner, look at the creator instead of the
organizer. This way, we won't falsely attribute the event ownership in secondary
calendars while keeping primary calendar behavior unchanged.

2. If an owner cannot be matched using the email (this will never be the case
for secondary calendars, see the email format in the example above), we will
assign the event to the calendar owner.

3. If a secondary calendar has no owner (eg. shared readonly calendar) the user
will simply be set to False

## Deleting calendars

When a synced calendar is deleted, we don't want to delete it on google. Doing
so would risk permanent loss of data with no recovery - the user can always
delete the calendar in google if they are sure (in that case, Odoo is not to
blame)

When we delete the calendar we delete all of it's records, and revert it to the
'ghost record' state before it was imported. This way the user can still recover
the calendar as long as it still exists on google.

